{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> This is a commandment of the heart, and it counts only as a positive commandment — that a person should acknowledge and believe in his heart with a complete faith that there exists one Creator, primordial, and there is none besides Him. And so the word אנכי points to that which exists (נמצא); and the א at the beginning points to unity and primordiality, and the rest of the letters are all tens (עשרות), which are divisions of the One; if so, the entire word in its entirety testifies to the Oneness. Furthermore, the word אנכי testifies to One, for אחד = 13 in squared value (מרובע); and furthermore the word אנכי comprises all the letters that are in the Torah, which are 22 — א from the three mothers, נ from the twelve simple letters, כ from the seven double letters — and this is the totality of all the letters, and the word is sealed with י, which is among the hidden ones (נעלמות)." "<b>And furthermore,</b> they said in the Midrash that in the word אנכ\"י there is a notarikon: אנא נפשאי כתבית יהבית — 'I myself wrote and gave My soul.'" "<b>And it is fitting</b> to illuminate the eyes of the heart with these four words, which allude that the Torah was given in the special Name. And you already know that every instance of אנכי said of the Holy One, blessed be He, Onkelos translated as מימרי — except this one. And He mentioned two names, the attribute of mercy and the attribute of judgment, for this commandment our Rabbis of blessed memory called 'acceptance of the yoke of the kingdom of Heaven,' and these attributes are indeed the attributes of a king toward the people: for the king must establish the land through justice, as it is said (Proverbs 29:4): ״מלך במשפט יעמיד ארץ״, and he must also deal with them in kindness, in the manner of what is written (ibid. 20): ״חסד ואמת יצרו מלך וסעד בחסד כסאו״, and it says: <b>who brought you out of the land of Egypt.</b> In order to provide proof of His divinity and the acceptance of His kingship from everything they saw with their own eyes and are able to testify about. And the intention is to say: you are indebted to Me to accept My divinity upon you, from what you saw with your own eyes — the signs and wonders included in the exodus from Egypt. And because of this He did not say 'who created the heavens and the earth,' for they did not see this with their own eyes; and He wanted to bring them proof of the divinity from what they themselves saw with their own eyes. Furthermore, the bringing out of the land of Egypt includes the creation of the world, for the wonders that followed that exodus are many, and from the fact that nature was altered in them, it is demonstrated from this the renewal of the world — for with primordiality nothing would change from its nature at all; and because of this He said 'who brought you out,' for surely the exodus indicated renewal and providence and power, that you should accept upon yourselves My divinity and My kingship, and that I should be Elohim to you and Master over you, and that you should serve Me and be servants to Me, just as I brought you out of the house of servants, from the house of the Egyptians, to whom you were servants. Or one may say 'from the house of servants' — that your masters were themselves servants, for the Egyptians are the children of Ham, as it is said (Genesis 10:6): ״ובני חם כוש ומצרים ופוט וכנען״, and you were servants to them. Or the word עבדים may be taken as a noun, as if it said 'from the house of servitude,' and similarly (ibid. 9): ״עבד עבדים יהיה לאחיו״, which means a servant for eternal servitude. And it is also possible to interpret 'from the house of servants' as 'from the house of the constellations (מזלות),' which are servants to Me — and He called the constellants 'servants' because they are kings over what is below them, being appointed over them, and servants to what is above them. And He alludes that Israel deserved to remain still longer in Egypt according to the judgments of the stars and constellations, were it not for 'HaShem who was for us' (לולא ה' שהיה לנו) — who humbled that force and brought them out by way of miracle. And this is what is written (Deuteronomy 4:20): ״ויוציא אתכם מכור הברזל ממצרים״ — He did not say 'from Egypt, the iron furnace,' because He compared the force of Egypt that is above to an iron furnace, in which Israel deserved to be poured like molten iron inside a furnace. And Scripture says that the Holy One, blessed be He, first brought them out of that iron furnace and afterward from Egypt — this is the meaning of 'מכור הברזל ממצרים.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:29.314762860Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}